findings.body:
The code saves the original setting in `orig_active_defrag` but never uses it. The restore line hardcodes `1`:

::diff{file="src/replication.c" lines="1953-1963"}

Take any replica that has `activedefrag no`, which is the default. After its first full sync, `server.active_defrag_enabled` becomes `1`, so active defrag starts running without the operator asking for it. `CONFIG GET activedefrag` will report `yes` too. On builds without jemalloc defrag support, this also gets around the `isValidActiveDefrag` check that normally refuses to enable it.

::callout{type="warning"}
The new test sets `activedefrag yes` before triggering the sync, so it can't catch this.

::suggestion{file="src/replication.c" lines="1962-1962"}
    server.active_defrag_enabled = orig_active_defrag;

The compiler would also likely warn that `orig_active_defrag` is set but never used.

The function's own contract says it returns 0 when the entry is fully acknowledged by all groups. At this point the code already knows that every group has read the entry (`id <= min_cgroup_last_id`). The remaining question is whether any PEL still holds it. If `cgroups_ref` is NULL, no PEL references exist at all, so the right answer is `0`:

::diff{file="src/t_stream.c" lines="2703-2711"}

Returning `1` stops the crash but breaks the ACKED strategy whenever `cgroups_ref` was never allocated. The two common cases are after an RDB load or `DEBUG RELOAD` with empty PELs, and on a group that has never had a pending entry. In those cases, `XADD/XTRIM ... ACKED` (the trim loop at line 848), `XACKDEL ... ACKED` and `XDELEX ... ACKED` all treat acknowledged entries as undeletable.

The new test in `tests/unit/type/stream.tcl` should fail with this code. After the second reload, entry 1-0 is acknowledged and has no references, so `MAXLEN = 1 ACKED` should trim it and leave XLEN = 2. With `return 1` it stays, and XLEN is 3.

::suggestion{file="src/t_stream.c" lines="2708-2708"}
    if (!s->cgroups_ref) return 0;

The original inline code drained replies only when `$count % 10000 == 0`. The helper uses `!= 0`:

::diff{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="70-76"}

With `!= 0`, the very first iteration (`count == 1`) tries to read 10,000 replies from the deferring client while only one is outstanding. `$rd read` blocks until the test times out. Every converted call site (big hash, string keys, list `lpush` with 20000, the slab tests) and the new replicaof defrag test go through this helper, so the whole jemalloc defrag suite is affected.

::suggestion{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="71-71"}
        if {$count % $frequency == 0} {

The test turns on `activedefrag yes` before `replicaof`, then checks only that defrag stops and `dbsize` is 0. Restoring the setting to the saved value and forcing it to `1` behave the same here, so the hardcoded `= 1` bug in `rdbLoadEmptyDbFunc` passes this test.

::code{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="981-1004"}

Add an assertion that `config get activedefrag` matches its value before the sync. Ideally also cover a case that starts with `activedefrag no`.
